oakMOSS

The files that are oakMOSS's own work (the scripts, the build configuration,
the documentation, the hand-off script, the container recipe and the patches
authored here) are licensed under the Creative Commons
Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International License (CC BY-NC 4.0), the same
terms spruceOS uses. You may copy, modify and share them for non-commercial
purposes with attribution. To view a copy of this license, visit
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/

This repository also carries, builds against, or downloads work by others;
those parts keep their own terms and are listed, with their origin, in
ATTRIBUTION.md. In particular:

- configs/phase1.config, configs/phase2.config, overlay/etc/rc.local and
  overlay/etc/banner are Shaun Inman's Moss-zero28 files, used with attribution
  (that repository carries no license file);
- overlay/usr/magicx/lib/ contains TrimUI's SDL2 builds (zlib-licensed libraries);
- sdk-patches/ncurses-6.2/ and sdk-patches/gcc-7.5.0-patches/ are OpenWrt
  material (GPL-2.0); the other patches under sdk-patches/ modify GPL/LGPL/ISC
  packages and are offered under the license of the package they touch;
- sdk-patches/tree/080-touch-hynitron-axs15205.patch carries a complete
  third-party kernel driver (GPL-2.0, see ATTRIBUTION.md) rather than a diff,
  and sdk-patches/sunxi_encrypt/encrypt_stub.c is oakMOSS's own GPL-2.0 kernel
  driver. The GPL-2.0 text is in LICENSES/GPL-2.0.txt, so anyone who receives
  this repository receives those terms with it;
- the Tina SDK, the Zero 40 kernel object, the Arm GNU Toolchain, OpenixCard and
  the main-zero40 release image are never stored here; the build downloads or
  expects them under their own terms.

A note on the built images. Nothing proprietary is stored in this repository,
but the images the build produces are a different matter: they contain the
Allwinner/MagicX kernel and U-Boot, Imagination's PowerVR GE8300 userland blobs,
the XR829 driver and firmware, the prebuilt SDL2 libraries above, and - in the
hybrid and stock-chain lanes - kernel modules and boot components taken from the
board's own stock firmware. Building those images for your own device is
ordinary use of a vendor SDK on vendor hardware. Publishing them redistributes
those vendor components, which this project does on the same footing as the rest
of the embedded-Linux ecosystem rather than under any explicit grant from the
vendors. Publishing a release here is therefore always a deliberate, manual
step. If a rights holder objects to any of it, it will be removed.

The images are provided as-is, without warranty of any kind. Flashing a device
replaces the firmware it boots from and is at your own risk.
